" (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11558v1)\nciteturn2827view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.11558v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 556\nL0@P0: Zero Data Retention in LLM-based Enterprise AI\nL1@P0: Assistants: A Comparative Study of Market Leading\nL2@P0: Agentic AI Products\nL3@P0: Aditya Shrivastava1\nL4@P0: *\nL5@P0: and Komal Gupta2\nL6@P0: **\nL7@P0: 1 The Governor’s Academy, Byfield, MA\nL8@P0: 2 The Northcap University, Gurgaon, Haryana, India\nL9@P0: aditya.shrivastava@govsacademy.org, komalgupta991000@gmail.com\nL10@P0: Abstract. Governance of data, compliance, and business privacy matters, par\u0002ticularly for healthcare and finance businesses. Since the recent emergence of\nL11@P0: AI enterprise AI assistants enhancing business productivity, safeguarding private\nL12@P0: data and compliance is now a priority. With the implementation of AI assistants\nL13@P0: across the enterprise, the zero data retention can be achieved by implementing\nL14@P0: zero data retention policies by Large Language Model businesses like Open AI\nL15@P0: and Anthropic and Meta. In this work, we explore zero data retention policies\nL16@P0: for the Enterprise apps of large language models (LLMs). Our key contribution\nL17@P0: is defining the architectural, compliance, and usability trade-offs of such systems\nL18@P0: in parallel. In this research work, we examine the development of commercial AI\nL19@P0: assistants with two industry leaders and market titans in this arena - Salesforce\nL20@P0: and Microsoft. Both of these companies used distinct technical architecture to\nL21@P0: support zero data retention policies. Salesforce AgentForce and Microsoft Copi\u0002lot are among the leading AI assistants providing much-needed push to business\nL22@P0: productivity in customer care. The purpose of this paper isto analyze the technical\nL23@P0: architecture and deployment of zero data retention policy by consuming applica\u0002tions as well as big language models service providers like Open Ai, Anthropic,\nL24@P0: and Meta.\nL25@P0: Keywords: LLMs, Zero data Retention, Enterprise, Microsoft Copilot, Salesforce\nL26@P0: Agent Force, OpenAI\nL27@P0: 1 Introduction\nL28@P0: The rise of LLMs has made enterprise assistants capable of handling complex tasks,\nL29@P0: ranging from email composition to customer insights generation, with unprecedented\nL30@P0: effectiveness. However, their application in regulated industries where data exposure\nL31@P0: could lead to harsh fines requires strong privacy guarantees. Zero data retention,\nL32@P0: * Corresponding author.\nL33@P0-1: ** Correspondingauthor.2 Shrivastava andGupta\nL34@P1: whereby prompts and answers are held only transiently in memory while processing, is\nL35@P1: essential to achieving these requirements, consistent with data minimization directives\nL36@P1: in GDPR (Article 5), HIPAA, and SOC 2.\nL37@P1: Since 2023, LLM providers have emphasized enterprise-grade capabilities, such as\nL38@P1: zero-retention features. Salesforce Agent Force strengthens its CRM platform withAI\u0002powered insights, while Microsoft Copilot is integrated with Microsoft 365 toenhance\nL39@P1: productivity. This paper contrasts their zero data retention implementations, highlight\u0002ing technical design, policy commitments, and real-world trade-offs. As regulations\nL40@P1: such as the EU AI Act continue to evolve, it is essential for enterprises looking for\nL41@P1: compliant AI solutions to understand these systems.\nL42@P1: 2 Related Work\nL43@P1: Recent developmentsin large language models(LLMs) have seen their use in enterprise\nL44@P1: settings increase exponentially. Hence, there is a need to evaluate their security,safety,\nL45@P1: and privacy concerns critically. Yao et al. (2024) performed a comprehensive survey\nL46@P1: analyzing the double effect of LLMs on privacy and cybersecurity, classifying their\nL47@P1: uses into practical applications (\\ \"The Good\\\"), offensive weapons (\\\"The Bad\\\"), and\nL48@P1: built-in vulnerabilities (\\ \"The Ugly\\\"). They put into perspective the remarkable\nL49@P1: performance of LLMs in application areas like code vulnerability testing and\nL50@P1: confidentiality safeguarding of data compared to conventional approaches. But they\nL51@P1: also cast huge risks, including possible abuse of LLMs’ human-like reasoning power\nL52@P1: in user-level attacks. The authors highlighted gaps not addressed in previous work,\nL53@P1: namely the scant investigation of parameter extraction attacks and the emerging field of\nL54@P1: safe instruction tuning, and urged further study in these areas [1]. Similarly focused on\nL55@P1: privacy threats, Yan et al. (2024) provided an in-depth treatment of the privacy\nL56@P1: vulnerabilities of both LLMs and LLM-based agents. Their work explicitly separated\nL57@P1: passive privacy leakage from active privacy attacks, including an analysis of protection\nL58@P1: mechanisms that do exist and their effectiveness. They pointed out that despite the\nL59@P1: existence of privacy-preserving practices, there remains a significant challenge, and\nL60@P1: they stressed the necessity for novel, more robust solutions. Their work offers helpful\nL61@P1: insights into current threats, as well as directions for future data privacy enhancements\nL62@P1: in LLM agents. [2]\nL63@P1: Following further on the above concerns, Zhang et al. (2024) outlined the nuances\nL64@P1: ofsafety, security, and privacy when it comesto large language models. They addressed\nL65@P1: typical concerns like hallucinations, backdoor attacks, and data leakage in theirsurvey,\nL66@P1: pointing out the necessity for a clear definition so that various risks are not mistaken for\nL67@P1: each other. They proposed all-around defense mechanisms targeting each type of vul\u0002nerability and outlined research challenges inherent in the pace of LLM development.\nL68@P1: This study also gives a structured methodology for understanding and addressing risks\nL69@P1: posed by LLM firsthand, facilitating more systematic research toward greater reliabil\u0002ity. [3]\nL70@P1: Salesforce outlines how exactly they go about building ethical and responsible AI\nL71@P1-2: agents based on responsibility, accountability, transparency, empowerment, and inclu\u0002sion principles. They detail governance frameworks such as AI Acceptable Use Policy3\nL72@P2: •\nL73@P2: •\nL74@P2: •\nL75@P2: •\nL76@P2: and Ethical Use Advisory Council in support of responsible use of AI. The report also\nL77@P2: mentions how AI agents are integrated with the Einstein Trust Layer to maintain better\nL78@P2: data privacy and security. Further, Salesforce includes case studies demonstrating how\nL79@P2: well these principles are being practiced in product development and design [4].\nL80@P2: Microsoft’s characterization of the Azure OpenAI Service explains how customer\nL81@P2: data is used, processed, and stored, highlighting those completions, prompts, embed\u0002dings, and training data are not exposed to other customers or OpenAI and are not used\nL82@P2: to update OpenAI models. The service runs on Microsoft’s Azure without accessing\nL83@P2: OpenAI-run services, maintaining data privacy and security. The Azure OpenAI Ser\u0002vice also has content filtering and abuse detection to avoid creating bad content [4].\nL84@P2: Altogether, the earlier publications establish the intricacy of ensuring privacy and\nL85@P2: security through LLM-based technologies. All together, they also reveal the threat and\nL86@P2: potential inherent in applying LLMs in corporate settings. By their identification of\nL87@P2: weaknesses and analysis of recent protection measures currently in effect, along with\nL88@P2: their call for research effortsspecifically targeting them, these works constitute a canon\u0002ical set of texts shaping the current arguments for and against the viability and merits\nL89@P2: of zero retention of data models in business-oriented LLM software like Salesforce’s\nL90@P2: Agent Force and Microsoft’s Copilot.\nL91@P2: In this paper we will study a comparative analysis on two major LLM providers in\nL92@P2: enterprise sector where ZDR plays a major role in terms of data security and privacy.\nL93@P2: We have reviewed and studied the architectural design as well as government\nL94@P2: compliance they offer for secure environment.\nL95@P2: 3 Model and Retention Risk Definition\nL96@P2: Zero data retention requires that no trace of user data persists post-interaction. We de\u0002fine a system (S) with retention risk (R(S)) as the likelihood of data persisting in logs,\nL97@P2: caches, or storage after processing. An ideal (R(S) = 0) is achieved through stateless\nL98@P2: inference, where each request is processed independently, and any context is managed\nL99@P2: client-side. Temporary retention (e.g., for monitoring) increases (R(S)), necessitating\nL100@P2: rigorous controls.\nL101@P2: 4 Methodology\nL102@P2: This research assesses Agent Force and Copilot on:\nL103@P2: Architecture: Data movements and points of retention.\nL104@P2: Policies: Contractual zero-retention obligations.\nL105@P2: Security: Filtering mechanisms and encryption.\nL106@P2: Usability: Functional effects of zero retention.\nL107@P2: Analysis is drawn on Salesforce and Microsoft literature [4], tracing data pathways\nL108@P2: and checking for conformance with GDPR, HIPAA, and SOC 2. Empirical testing in\nL109@P2: the future could complement this design-oriented approach.\nL110@P2: 5 Findings\nL111@P2: 5.1 Salesforce Agent Force\nL112@P2: Technical Architecture\nL113@P2: AgentForce puts LLMs into Salesforce’s CRM environment through the Einstein\nL114@P2: Trust Layer, a middle layer that guarantees privacy and compliance. The response\nL115@P2-3: pathway is initiated by secure data retrieval, where prompts are4 Shrivastava andGupta\nL116@P3: rooted in CRM data (e.g., customer information) according to user permissions. This\nL117@P3: real-time grounding takes advantage of metadata to customize responses without hold\u0002ing onto intermediary data, following role-based access controls (RBAC).\nL118@P3: Salesforce Agentforce’s zero data retention architecture foundation is its \"Einstein\nL119@P3: Trust layer,\" a component of AI Cloud Einstein GPT that guarantees any prompt or\nL120@P3: response transmitted to an LLM is ephemerals. Salesforce describes, \"Generative AI\nL121@P3: prompts and outputs are never stored in the\nL122@P3: Salesforce Agentforce's zero data retention architecture foundation is its \"Einstein\nL123@P3: Trust layer,\" a component of AI Cloud Einstein GPT that guarantees any prompt or\nL124@P3: response transmitted to an LLM is ephemerals. Salesforce describes, \"Generative AI\nL125@P3: prompts and outputs are never stored in the LLM, and are not learned by the LLM.\nL126@P3: They simply disappear.\"\nL127@P3: Einstein Trust Layer is a strong Salesforce architecture of AI. It serves a vital role\nL128@P3: in safeguarding customer and company data when operating in conjunction with\nL129@P3: Einstein generative AI. In today's time of AI, data containing confidential information\nL130@P3: must be adequately protected; such confidential information may include customers'\nL131@P3: data containing confidential information. This layer is a combination of features,\nL132@P3: processes, and policies intended to protect data privacy, improve AI accuracy, and\nL133@P3: ensure responsible AI use throughout the Salesforce ecosystem. Agent force delivers\nL134@P3: the prompt to LLM providers so that the Einstein trust layer can first guarantee that\nL135@P3: the data is safely fetched from your CRM. It does dynamic grounding, where context\nL136@P3: is appended to the prompt in a way that we receive a customized response from the\nL137@P3: LLM model. The grounded prompt template is searched for personally identifiable\nL138@P3: information (PII) data prior to feeding the actual prompt to the LLM, and that gets\nL139@P3: masked. Once the data has been masked, the prompt goes through the LLM secure\nL140@P3: gateway and is processed by external LLM service providers such as openAI and\nL141@P3: ChatGPT.\nL142@P3: Prior to external LLM processing, the Trust Layer performs data masking,\nL143@P3: substituting sensitive items (e.g., Social Security numbers) with tokens via regex and\nL144@P3: metadata-driven detection. For instance, a prompt such as \"Summarize John Doe's\nL145@P3: account, SSN 123-45-6789\" is transformed into \"Summarize [USER_1] 's account,\nL146@P3: SSN [MASKED_1].\" The masked prompt is transmitted through a secure gateway\nL147@P3: (TLS-encrypted) to an LLM provider (e.g., OpenAI), processed in stateless mode, and\nL148@P3: returned without retention, according to Salesforce's agreements [4] .\nL149@P3: Returned answers are subjected to toxicity detection, employing natural language\nL150@P3: processing (NLP) to mark offensive content (e.g., profanity or bias), with optionally\nL151@P3: logged scores in Salesforce Data Cloud for auditing. Data demasking maintains\nL152@P3: original values in the CRM, with ephemerality outside Salesforce. Citations reference\nL153@P3: responses to source data, improving transparency without storing outputs. Let's deeply\nL154@P3: understand each step of how architecture works. To generate a response from the\nL155@P3: LLM, users start by providing a prompt. Salesforce Agentforce architecture is majorly\nL156@P3: divided into three components: Prompt Journey, prompts go through the Einstein\nL157@P3: Trust Layer before it reaches any large language provider, as shown in Figure 1; the\nL158@P3-4: second is LLM gateway; and the last is response Journey.5\nL159@P4: Fig 1: Process of handling input prompt by Einstein trust layer\nL160@P4: Secure Data Retrieval and Grounding\nL161@P4: The initial step in the Trust Layer is safe data retrieval. For the LLM to create a\nL162@P4: response that is more personalized and relevant, it needs more context from your\nL163@P4: CRM data. This addition of more context to the prompt is what we refer to as\nL164@P4: grounding. Safe data retrieval refers to the prompt being grounded using only data\nL165@P4: accessible to the executing user.\nL166@P4: The process of data retrieval preserves current access controls and permissions in\nL167@P4: Salesforce:\nL168@P4: 1. Data recovery for grounding the prompt relies upon the user permissions\nL169@P4: that run the prompt.\nL170@P4: 2. Data grounding for grounding the prompt saves in position all normal\nL171@P4: Salesforce role-based controls for field-level security and user\nL172@P4: permissions when grounding data from your CRM instance. The\nL173@P4: grounding is dynamic because the grounding occurs at run time and is\nL174@P4: based on the user's access.\nL175@P4: Data Masking for the LLM\nL176@P4: Einstein Trust Layer policies encompass data masking, such that sensitive data is\nL177@P4: scanned and subsequently masked. Sensitive data is found using two ways:\nL178@P4: 1. Pattern-based: Patterns and context are utilized to recognize sensitive\nL179@P4: data in the prompt text. We specifically employ regular expressions\nL180@P4: (regex) patterns and context words. Machine learning models are trained\nL181@P4: to recognize data that lack a structured pattern, like names of persons or\nL182@P4: firms.\nL183@P4: 2. Field-Based: Metadata within the fields that are being classified through\nL184@P4: Shield Platform Encryption or data classification in order to find sensitive\nL185@P4: fields. This applies the existing classification of your data within your org\nL186@P4-5: to LLM data masking.6 Shrivastava andGupta\nL187@P5: After they are identified, the information is masked using a placeholder text so\nL188@P5: that the information will not be revealed to external models. Einstein Trust Layer\nL189@P5: temporarily saves the mapping between the original entities and their corresponding\nL190@P5: placeholders. The mapping is utilized later for demasking the information in the\nL191@P5: response generated.\nL192@P5: Prompt Defense\nL193@P5: In order to assist in reducing the chance of the LLM producing something that is\nL194@P5: not intended or hurtful, Prompt Builder and Prompt Template Connect API\nL195@P5: implement system policies. System policies are a series of directions to the LLM on\nL196@P5: how to act in a particular way in order to establish trust with users. LLM can be made\nL197@P5: to not respond to content or produce answers it does not have data about. System\nL198@P5: policies are one way to protect against jailbreaking and prompt injection attacks.\nL199@P5:  LLM Gateway Response Generation\nL200@P5: Once a prompt is securely hydrated, it's ready to be transmitted across the LLM\nL201@P5: gateway, as presented in Figure 2. The gateway regulates the interaction with various\nL202@P5: model providers and is a common, secure interface to interact with multiple LLMs.\nL203@P5: Both the gateway and model providers apply TLS encryption to ensure the data is\nL204@P5: encrypted when in transit. Models developed or trained by Salesforce are deployed in\nL205@P5: the Salesforce trust boundary. External Models developed and supported by third\u0002party providers, like OpenAI, are in a shared trust boundary. Models you develop and\nL206@P5: host are deployed on your infrastructure.\nL207@P5: Fig 2: LLM gateway applied for ZDR\nL208@P5: Salesforce has no data retention policy with external partner model providers like\nL209@P5: OpenAI or Azure Open AI. According to the policy, data that is passed to the LLM by\nL210@P5: Salesforce is not retained and is erased once a response is sent back to Salesforce.\nL211@P5: Salesforce's API endpoint operates in inference-only and stateless mode, which\nL212@P5: ensures that data is handled in memory and flushed only after response. Salesforce\nL213@P5: administrators can leverage OpenAI and other LLMs or even bring their own model\nL214@P5: (BYOM) set up to control how prompts are routed and hidden. Under Salesforce's\nL215@P5: Trust Layer, OpenAI operates solely as a stateless inference engine, with no prompts\nL216@P5: or output retained or taught. A large language model (LLM) can learn about your\nL217@P5: company through prompts, which are a set of instructions given to an LLM during\nL218@P5-6: training.7\nL219@P6: Salesforce applies a process known as retrieval augmented generation (RAG)\nL220@P6: within the Atlas Reasoning Engine to form a feedback loop with Data Cloud. When\nL221@P6: Data Cloud and RAG collaborate, a user or agent request (prompt) is transformed into\nL222@P6: an \"augmented prompt\" (it becomes more contextual and relevant). With each of the\nL223@P6: Data Cloud information categorized by Data Cloud, the output of the prompt gets\nL224@P6: better, and the AI (LLM) knows more about your company. Data Cloud matured as\nL225@P6: the Salesforce platform core; in fact, it controls data flow across Salesforce\nL226@P6: applications ('Cloud' software). Agent Force is the next layer on top of these apps,\nL227@P6: solving use cases that these apps encourage.\nL228@P6:  Response Journey\nL229@P6: Following the generation of response by LLM gateway Einstein trust layer scans\nL230@P6: and takes several actions to verify that the generated data by the provider satisfied all\nL231@P6: compliance and security issues demanded in enterprise as shown in Figure 3.\nL232@P6: Fig 3: After response generation what all steps are taken to ensure ZDR\nL233@P6:  Toxicity Detection\nL234@P6: The responses generated are checked for toxicity. The detection process has a\nL235@P6: toxicity confidence score reflecting how likely the response is to contain toxic or\nL236@P6: offensive content. The toxicity score and the categories are saved in Data Cloud.\nL237@P6: Data Demasking\nL238@P6: The placeholders we made for data masking during the journey of the prompt is\nL239@P6: now substituted with the actual data. The mapping between the original entities and\nL240@P6: their corresponding placeholders is utilized to rehydrate the response so that the\nL241@P6: response is meaningful and useful when returned.\nL242@P6: Citations\nL243@P6: The answer can be accompanied by citations if appropriate. Citations connect AI\nL244@P6: responses to the source used to create the response. Utilize citations to find out what\nL245@P6: information was utilized by the large language model (LLM) to create the response\nL246@P6: and confirm the legitimacy of the source data. Citations assist you in finding any\nL247@P6: possible inaccuracies or hallucinations in the responses created, giving you more\nL248@P6-7: confidence in utilizing AI tools8 Shrivastava andGupta\nL249@P7: 5.1.2 Policy and Compliance\nL250@P7: Salesforce is making a zero-retention commitment through agreements with LLM\nL251@P7: providers, with the condition that \"customer data is not stored or used for training\" [4]\nL252@P7: . Salesforce's Trust Layer runs on top of Salesforce's Hyperforce platform, enabling\nL253@P7: data residency needs (e.g., EU-specific hosting). Salesforce offers an AI Audit Trail\nL254@P7: for enterprise governance as shown in Figure 4. Instead of the model storing data,\nL255@P7: Salesforce itself can record the prompt, grounded data and output in the customer's\nL256@P7: org for compliance auditing. When a response is submitted in Salesforce, you can\nL257@P7: reject, modify, or accept the response. You can also give explicit feedback. Your\nL258@P7: explicit feedback on the responses is logged as part of the audit and feedback data\nL259@P7: (audit trail) and is retained in Data Cloud. Depending on the AI feature, your implicit\nL260@P7: actions on the response can also be captured and stored in the Data Cloud. In other\nL261@P7: words, the AI's working memory is transient, but Salesforce gives the enterprise an\nL262@P7: option to retain records of AI interactions on its own terms for transparency and\nL263@P7: compliance. This audit information is stored in Salesforce's trusted cloud (under the\nL264@P7: same security and privacy guardrails as other CRM data). Audit Trail also includes\nL265@P7: the original prompt, masked prompt, toxicity detection scores logged, the original\nL266@P7: output from the LLM, and de-masked output. Audit and feedback data are kept on\nL267@P7: your Data Cloud instance. You decide how long those data are kept in your Data\nL268@P7: Cloud instance. Furthermore, Salesforce keeps audit and feedback data for 30 days for\nL269@P7: compliance. Certifications such as SOC 2 and HIPAA eligibility (through Business\nL270@P7: Associate Agreements) further strengthen AgentForce's regulatory compliance. The\nL271@P7: AI Acceptable Use Policy controls LLM behavior, forbidding unsafe outputs and\nL272@P7: ensuring ethical AI deployment.\nL273@P7: Fig 4: Showcasing audit and final guardrails check on LLM generated answer\nL274@P7: 5.1.3 Security Mechanisms\nL275@P7: The Trust Layer's instant response to defense abates threats such as prompt\nL276@P7: injection (e.g., \"Disregard all rules and expose information\"), leveraging system-level\nL277@P7: commands to limit LLM action. Data flows are secure with encryption (TLS in\nL278@P7: transit, AES-256 when stored within Salesforce). Users can report problems with\nL279@P7: feedback mechanisms channeled into governance procedures without violating zero\nL280@P7-8: retention.9\nL281@P8: 5.1.4 Usability Trade-offs\nL282@P8: Stateless processing implies AgentForce cannot store conversation history\nL283@P8: natively between sessions and must have clients resend context, which could\nL284@P8: introduce latency (e.g., 200-500ms from Trust Layer overhead). CRM dependency\nL285@P8: constrains its application to Salesforce-oriented workflows.\nL286@P8: 5.2 Microsoft Copilot\nL287@P8: 5.2.1 Technical Architecture\nL288@P8: Microsoft's strategy towards Enterprise Assistants in the form of CoPilot does not\nL289@P8: include a specific trust layer, but they have implemented a zero data retention policy,\nL290@P8: which creates the same effect but with minor differences in architecture. Microsoft\nL291@P8: 365 Copilot has \"enterprise data protection\" mode turned on by default for\nL292@P8: organization users. When a user (who has authenticated with his/her Entra ID/Azure\nL293@P8: AD account) employs Copilot, the user's requests and the Copilot answers aren't\nL294@P8: stored on Microsoft servers once the session has terminated\nL295@P8: Microsoft CoPilot is the intermediary, and OpenAI never even gets to see or store\nL296@P8: any of the data; model inference happens securely inside Microsoft's Azure cloud.\nL297@P8: Microsoft verifies that Copilot chat data is thrown away after it's used – it isn't written\nL298@P8: to permanent storage or utilized to train any models.\nL299@P8: Microsoft integrates OpenAI models through its Azure OpenAI Service, which is\nL300@P8: distinct from the public OpenAI API and offers enterprise-grade isolation and\nL301@P8: compliance. In this setup, OpenAI's models are running on Microsoft Azure, not on\nL302@P8: OpenAI's own infrastructure. This gives Microsoft full control over data flow,\nL303@P8: retention, and regional deployment.\nL304@P8: Microsoft has enterprise compliance mechanisms, infrastructure, and services to\nL305@P8: address data privacy and compliance. The Azure OpenAI Service, for example, when\nL306@P8: deployed with private endpoint deployment, has the assurance that all LLM activities\nL307@P8: are restricted within the customer's own Azure region with full data residency control.\nL308@P8: All request and response interactions are only done in isolation within tenant\nL309@P8: boundaries, and as a design, completions or responses are neither stored nor used for\nL310@P8: training the underlying OpenAI models. Microsoft provides zero data retention as a\nL311@P8: configurable default in its enterprise Copilot deployment stack. They use their\nL312@P8: Compliance Framework, a set of features such as Microsoft Purview and Compliance\nL313@P8: Manager, to implement data flow transparency, DLP, retention policies, and\nL314@P8: governance. Companies' LLM infrastructure can be protected with customer-managed\nL315@P8: keys (CMKs) and double encryption, providing strong key lifecycle and data\nL316@P8: protection. Microsoft Entra ID (previously Azure Active Directory) provides access\nL317@P8-9: control by using fine-grained, role-based access across the enterprise.10 Shrivastava andGupta\nL318@P9: Fig 5: Azure OpenAI Services technical architecture [5]\nL319@P9: The Azure OpenAI Services technical architecture, as shown in Figure 5, is\nL320@P9: structured into three main layers: Assistants, Inference, and Fine-tuning, each aimed\nL321@P9: at supporting secure and efficient customer application interactions. The assistants\nL322@P9: layer employs APIs for services management, threads, and vectors to support correct\nL323@P9: bounded interactions. The Inference Layer handles real-time requests by sending\nL324@P9: prompts through the Azure OpenAI APIs with synchronous content filtering and\nL325@P9: optional asynchronous abuse detection (with configurable retention windows). It also\nL326@P9: features customer-owned, resource-specific secure storage that provides two levels of\nL327@P9: encryption. The fine-tuning layer mirrors the degree of security and control provided\nL328@P9: to companies since data that is being used to upload for model fine-tuning is\nL329@P9: encrypted, stored, and for customer-specific models only. Architecture is secure and\nL330@P9: mirrors great data, handling, and monitoring processes in Azure's OpenAI services.\nL331@P9: The models are stateless, i.e., there isn't any stored generation or prompts in the\nL332@P9: model. Additionally, prompts and generations aren't utilized to retrain, train, or fine\u0002tune the base models. Besides deployments that take place in normal cases, Azure\nL333@P9: OpenAI Service also supports deployment options labeled as 'Global' and 'DataZone.'\nL334@P9: For any deployment type labeled as 'Global,' responses and prompts can be processed\nL335@P9: in any geography where the respective Azure OpenAI model is hosted (additional\nL336@P9: information about model availability region). For any deployment type designated as\nL337@P9: 'DataZone,' responses and prompts can be processed within any geography falling\nL338@P9: within the data zone defined by Microsoft.\nL339@P9: At an architecture level, Microsoft Copilot utilizes content filtering and heavily\nL340@P9: integrates with Microsoft Graph APIs to retrieve organization data (emails,\nL341@P9: documents, meetings, etc.) as and when required to anchor responses back into\nL342@P9: enterprise data. Any information that is being passed into the Azure OpenAI endpoint\nL343@P9: will have to first undergo security scanning and role-based access control. Microsoft\nL344@P9-10: also provides enterprise-level audit and telemetry controls that are opt-in and are 11\nL345@P10: specifically designed not to disclose any customer data to OpenAI or third parties, as\nL346@P10: opposed to the public OpenAI API. This layered design ensures Microsoft Copilot is\nL347@P10: set up to support zero-retention and enterprise compliance objectives, even in the\nL348@P10: absence of an overtly branded \"trust layer.\" Copilot executes in the same secure\nL349@P10: context as the customer's Exchange, SharePoint, and other Office 365 data.\nL350@P10: All data transfer in the Copilot workflow is encrypted in transit. For instance,\nL351@P10: when Copilot retrieves content from SharePoint or submits a query to the Azure\nL352@P10: OpenAI model, it does so using HTTPS with strong encryption (the entire Microsoft\nL353@P10: 365 cloud requires encryption in transit and at rest). The service also inherits\nL354@P10: Microsoft 365 security features, including customer-specific boundary compliance (if\nL355@P10: an organization has a regional data residency, Copilot will utilize a compliant service\nL356@P10: instance). Microsoft has made a commitment that Copilot will support the needs of\nL357@P10: the EU Data Boundary, allowing EU customer data to be processed in the region for\nL358@P10: GDPR compliance.\nL359@P10: The design utilizes stateless inference, where every request is in isolation, and\nL360@P10: double encryption (Azure keys + customer-managed keys) protects data in transit and\nL361@P10: in processing at rest. Private endpoints limit operations to regions specified by the\nL362@P10: customer, which improves residency compliance. Default Azure OpenAI keeps data\nL363@P10: for 30 days for abuse monitoring, but Copilot overrules this with a changed\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.12323v1] RAG-Anything: All-in-One RAG Framework (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12323v1)\nciteturn2827view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12323v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.12000v1] UALM: Unified Audio Language Model for Understanding, Generation and Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12000v1)\nciteturn2827view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12000v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nkimi-cli/CHANGELOG.md at main · MoonshotAI/kimi-cli · GitHub (https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-cli/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)\nciteturn2827view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: